{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay","id":1406183,"title":"Trying to understand fay","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-08-03T16:56:49-07:00","id":1406183,"title":"Trying to understand fay","content":"I wanted to use a fay in a scenario, probably the manipulative kind. <br /> <br /> I managed to get my hands on some fluff, but don't quite get the feel of how they should work mechanically (the 2E stuff revolves around character traits that no longer exist, and 3E only gave us a couple of bestiary entries.)<br /> <br /> From what I see they look more or less like this:<br /> <br /> Creatues from the primal chaos, stuck into more or less stable forms, in a love-hate relationship with reality. <br /> Very high stats that are very hard to further improve.<br /> Don't lie. (why? do they need to remain true to themselves to maintain a stable 'self'?) <br /> Feed on personality traits by making their victims turn back on them (seducing the pious, scaring the brave, etc.) gradually lobotomising the victim in the process. (how would that work in 3E? would they erode principles?) <br /> Super rich, capable of spinning dreams into gold if they need to.<br /> Obsessed with narrative devices, as they are the most intuitive thing for them in reality. Try to pusposefully become Mary Sue. <br /> <br /> - What happens if they are killed in Creation? Does it just kill them, or do they need to be finished off in some special way?<br /> - Where are they power level-wise compared to exalted?<br /> - Beyond the abilities described in the bestiary examples, are there any other iconic things they should be able to do? <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23788-braininthejar","name":"Braininthejar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-03T23:29:40-07:00","id":1406227,"title":"","content":"If killed in Creation, that particular Raksha is dead. A different fey might take up their story though, killing who they were to become the dead fey. They could possess healing abilities that make them hard to kill without iron, but as of 3E they probably don't.<br /> <br /> Fey nobles (Loralie, Cataphract) are a little bit above a starting Dragon-Blooded but below a starting Solar.<br /> <br /> I think you've described the iconic things fey do in your post. They're formless monsters spun into stories by the power of reality. They have whatever powers the story needs them to have. There's fey in Adversaries of the Righteous if you wanted to expand your reading.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-04T03:05:50-07:00","id":1406244,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay#post1406183\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Don't lie. (why? do they need to remain true to themselves to maintain a stable 'self'?)</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I'm not sure if honesty is or ever was a part of the raksha; Geoff Grabowski described them as being outright made from lies. Are you maybe thinking of how they're bound to keep their promises?</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Feed on personality traits by making their victims turn back on them (seducing the pious, scaring the brave, etc.) gradually lobotomising the victim in the process. (how would that work in 3E? would they erode principles?)</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Honestly, I don't think it really needs to target something specific on the character sheet. For the moment I think it's enough to just run with a story that extended proximity to a raksha and acting in the manner most responsive to them weakens one's soul in a manner that affects personality or gradually reduces motive and motion, while the raksha is sustained in Creation by it.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Obsessed with narrative devices, as they are the most intuitive thing for them in reality. Try to pusposefully become Mary Sue.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I think the narrative device thing is played out, and that it's better to look at a raksha as being focused on a mythic or mystical vision of themselves or of the world that all efforts are focused on bringing to life. The &quot;become Mary Sue&quot; thing in particular ought not to be pursued, because it's a bit of a caricature and probably cuts off a lot of interesting raksha ideas. Like, imagine a creepy self-flagellating mendicant monk who goes around with a flail of iron, obsessed with being punished for his sins real and imaginary while purging them in a manner that causes them to rise personified from the ground where the blood from his back is sprinkled. I would find that in keeping with our example nobles, and I wouldn't call that trying to become a Mary Sue.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406227#post1406227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Fey nobles (Loralie, Cataphract) are a little bit above a starting Dragon-Blooded but below a starting Solar.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the lorelei and cataphract may actually just be the equivalent of commoners, even if that term might not be used anymore. They don't seem as elaborately wrought as the ones in Fangs at the Gate or Adversaries of the Righteous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-04T03:44:56-07:00","id":1406248,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406244#post1406244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think the lorelei and cataphract may actually just be the equivalent of commoners, even if that term might not be used anymore. They don't seem as elaborately wrought as the ones in Fangs at the Gate or Adversaries of the Righteous.</div> </div> </div> </div>He was talking in term of stats power level I think ?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-04T05:01:57-07:00","id":1406253,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406248#post1406248\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> He was talking in term of stats power level I think ?</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually it's a fair point. Whilst one can certainly invent a grand backstory for your Cataphract or Lorelei, Adeimantus has &quot;Raksha senators equivalent to cataphracts and lorelei&quot; as his Retainers.<br /> <br /> That said, in terms of combat prowess and social acumen he's very comparable to a lorelei. The lorelei is attacking for 12 Accuracy and 11 Damage to Adeimantus's 11/11. The lorelei is throwing &quot;Instill or <br /> Persuade: 13 dice&quot; to Adeimantus's Charisma 7 Presence 5. Resolve/Guile of 5/7 vs 6/6.<br /> <br /> The commoner/noble distinction might not exist in 3E in the same manner as in 2E. But I think Adeimantus represents something more like a powerful, unusual Lorelei than a whole different class of being. That said, as Isator expands below, fey nobility still exists in some form as evidenced in the Raksha Corebook write-ups. But my point, as Chausse rightly deduced, is that in straight interaction with the PCs fey nobels aren't meaningfully more challenging. Just as Essence 6+ Exalts develop personalised, unique powers rather than becoming more powerful as a flat upgrade, so too does it seem that Raksha nobles are characterised by their unique quirks rather than &quot;more dice&quot; -- and thus remain at the level of &quot;above Dragon-Blooded but below Solars&quot;. <br /> <br /> By contrast, the fey created by Marching Fever Dream Nightmare, Hobgoblins and Silverwrights seem meaningfully weaker and more limited. Much more like the old commoners. But YMMV.<br /> <br /> (Which fey are in Fangs?)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 08-05-2020, 07:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-04T05:26:14-07:00","id":1406256,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406244#post1406244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"> Like, imagine a creepy self-flagellating mendicant monk who goes around with a flail of iron, obsessed with being punished for his sins real and imaginary while purging them in a manner that causes them to rise personified from the ground where the blood from his back is sprinkled. I would find that in keeping with our example nobles, and I wouldn't call that trying to become a Mary Sue.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. You look like you could poke my idea in the right direction. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> My basic idea for the adventure was like this: the ruler of a desert city is throwing a party to celebrate his son's coming of age. A young girl from the city dreams of going to the party but is not nearly on the level of wealth that would allow it - and a rahska decides to 'help' her, but of course it can't just be a party. She needs it to be a love story. My initial idea was &quot;people mind-controlled into re-enacting Cinderella&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now I need to tweak this idea a bit - first because there are a couple dragon-blooded likely to attend and I need to plan how their possible intervention could affect the plot - also you seem like you could have some ideas for making the whole thing more unique and interesting. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23788-braininthejar","name":"Braininthejar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-04T05:54:48-07:00","id":1406257,"title":"","content":"In terms of power level it's actually quite useful for them to be absolutely all over the place, and mostly as powerful as their narrative role demands them to be. Exploring that range is pretty useful to make them feel more integral to the setting (especially since bordermarches can appear just about anywhere). This is how I like to stratify them:<br /> <br /> <br /> From the tiniest trickster spirits that pull pranks and entice children to dangerous places and can be scared off by a stick to the dangerous predators that sniff nightmares and enter unprotected houses at night you have your goblins and hobgoblins.<br /> These tend to be weak, and most mortal warriors should at least have a good shot at tackling them (a small militia can take on a few hobgoblins or a couple silverwights, but it's deadly).<br /> <br /> From the gallant mermaid luring fishermen to love-devour them to the Road Warrior that tags along caravans to protect them from bandits and wilderbeasts free of charge (and the beasts likely were put there by his partner in crime) are your raksha proper and the meaner beasts you'd start calling &quot;monsters&quot;. A starting combative exalt should make quick work of them, but underestimating them will hurt. <br /> These should be the most you'll find in any given trek near the marches. <br /> <br /> Raksha nobles often have their own entourages of lesser fae, and have earned many a wyld hunt themselves. Nobles have a gravitas to them that kinda takes over the story. Either you are trekking deep to find them or they are on a rampage, and can offer wonders of glamour that might as well be called miracles. They are the warlock in the woods whose songs cure Flamerot; the rampaging warlord wiping out multiple villages before disappearing into the ocean; the keeper of a fortress that can only be safely visited by the pure of heart; the queen so beautiful you have to give up your eyes as tribute as soon as your gaze lands on her form. These guys go as high up as a second circle demon in power level, though usually it's less than that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-04T14:32:03-07:00","id":1406329,"title":"","content":"My overall impression from 3e corebook is that the idea of Nobles and Commoners - and more importantly, one kind of being eventually evolving into the other - are gone.<br /> <br /> Raksha now resume themselves to everything that we would put under the &quot;fae noble&quot; in previous editions, the Cataphract being a hint of that on itself.<br /> <br /> The idea of &quot;commoners&quot; has been replaced with masses of varied inhuman beings, created or native to the Wyld, taken as bedazzled servants, disgruntled slaves and more, that the Creationborn, specially mortals might classify out of gauche ignorance as &quot;fae&quot; along with their masters, <i>but are in no way Raksha</i>.<br /> <br /> Goblins, silverwights and more compose the ranks of such chimeric beasts - and might form bands and maybe even whole societies of their own with particular agendas and interests independent any Raksha or other groups of oppressors. That said, bands of some of those beings may do the opposite and flock of their own volition to some sufficiently charismatic, puisant or strong-willed being that attracts their attention.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay#post1406183\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Feed on personality traits by making their victims turn back on them (seducing the pious, scaring the brave, etc.) gradually lobotomising the victim in the process. (how would that work in 3E? would they erode principles?)</div> </div> </div> </div>My quick-&amp;-dirty provisory take of the subject is, roughly speaking to use Intimacies in place of Virtues/Graces+1 for matters of bonuses or resistance, what while not a perfect match to the 1-5 range of virtues or graces, does give most people a 1-4 range that could work well enough for a quickport/stopgap solution, i guess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 08-04-2020, 05:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T02:07:58-07:00","id":1406401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406253#post1406253\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> (Which fey are in Fangs?)</div> </div> </div> </div>Spear-Empress Bhadri. Prince Gelyb, the Song of Fire. Prince Mharidan of the Storm Kettle. Prince Zalak of the Wave-Cutter Chariot. Maja of the Starling Song.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406329#post1406329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My overall impression from 3e corebook is that the idea of Nobles and Commoners - and more importantly, one kind of being eventually evolving into the other - are gone.<br /> <br /> Raksha now resume themselves to everything that we would put under the &quot;fae noble&quot; in previous editions, the Cataphract being a hint of that on itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>The cataphract write-up says &quot;Among their own kind, most swear themselves to the service of raksha nobles. Some ride as captains and generals amidst armies of lesser Fair Folk. Others are armsmasters, bodyguards, or masters of the hunt&quot;.<br /> <br /> The lorelei write-up says &quot;Others serve as courtiers in the freeholds of the Fair Folk. There they dance and preen, carouse and gossip, scheme and intrigue, all in the service of the raksha nobility. These serve as their masters' playthings, but the leash of addiction and the reins of desire can reverse this role, bending the master to the lorelei's whim&quot;.<br /> <br /> Their distinction from a nobility is stated outright, and they are designated in roles of service (although the lorelei description offers the point that they are not merely servile, and can hold a measure of power over their lieges).<br /> <br /> As for evolution, if Adeimantus is slain then one of his retainers might take control of his city and assume a similar power of insinuating into Creation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 08-05-2020, 05:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T02:28:31-07:00","id":1406403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406256#post1406256\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My basic idea for the adventure was like this: the ruler of a desert city is throwing a party to celebrate his son's coming of age. A young girl from the city dreams of going to the party but is not nearly on the level of wealth that would allow it - and a rahska decides to 'help' her, but of course it can't just be a party. She needs it to be a love story. My initial idea was &quot;people mind-controlled into re-enacting Cinderella&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that idea, of a raksha whose obsession concerns spinning these grand romances that cause social upheaval, I would find that perfectly fitting. Matches the commoner with the prince, star-struck teenagers from feuding houses, a third iconic doomed romance that I thought of when contemplating this yesterday and that I should have written down... probably wasn't Helen of Troy.<br /> <br /> Are they doing it to be helpful, truly heedless of the inevitable disasters that ensue when realpolitik is injected? Hard to say; the raksha are made of lies.<br /> <br /> I think it's perfectly suitable, necessary even, to draw on inspiration from broad, archetypal stories to find what any given raksha is about; it just goes a step too far to have them consciously, deliberately acting in emulation of stories. I like the idea that there's ultimately a measure of artifice and play-acting to what they do, particularly in relation to one another, but it takes me out of it when an intro fiction has them stand up and describe that artifice outright.<br /> <br /> As for your character, I would have reservations about outright mind control being involved, that the people playing their parts are purely puppets, if only because I think it takes a lot of the fun out of it. I think the powers of the raksha should be concerned with creating incentives, contriving scenarios, laying down glamour and enchantment, all to guide people towards assuming the roles and working through their conclusions of their own accord. It is the nature of such things that the more people immerse themselves in the fantasy, the more they'll lose of themselves, and <i>eventually</i> much of their wills shall be in the holding of the raksha, but it happens after they've ceded much of themselves up to the dream that is more compelling than reality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now I need to tweak this idea a bit - first because there are a couple dragon-blooded likely to attend and I need to plan how their possible intervention could affect the plot </div> </div> </div> </div>I expect that their intervention can present a decent risk to the raksha's designs, with their competing powers to sway the hearts and minds of others and to disrupt faerie magic with the stabilising influence of Earth, not to mention their person. One Fair Folk might look upon such people as unwanted competition or an obstacle to their godchild's happiness or other such thing and wish to try removing them before the major proceedings even start. But the other will see the presence of the Exalted as a grand prize worth risking much for, and try to work towards their lending their power and status to aid the rise of the godchild. Try to present the Cindarella as a person of surpassing purity of character, such that these Dragon-Blooded pledge their fealty and swear to see such a fine heart placed at the side of the throne, for the good of the country.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T07:12:55-07:00","id":1406430,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406401#post1406401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The cataphract write-up says &quot;Among their own kind, most swear themselves to the service of raksha nobles. Some ride as captains and generals amidst armies of lesser Fair Folk. Others are armsmasters, bodyguards, or masters of the hunt&quot;.<br /> <br /> The lorelei write-up says &quot;Others serve as courtiers in the freeholds of the Fair Folk. There they dance and preen, carouse and gossip, scheme and intrigue, all in the service of the raksha nobility. These serve as their masters' playthings, but the leash of addiction and the reins of desire can reverse this role, bending the master to the lorelei's whim&quot;.<br /> <br /> Their distinction from a nobility is stated outright, and they are designated in roles of service (although the lorelei description offers the point that they are not merely servile, and can hold a measure of power over their lieges).<br /> <br /> As for evolution, if Adeimantus is slain then one of his retainers might take control of his city and assume a similar power of insinuating into Creation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Terminology can be hell sometimes, i guess. The term noble when it comes up in 3e seems to apply only to the ruling lord or lady of a Raksha court.<br /> Such was <b>not</b> the case in previous editions, where Cataphractoi, Eshu, Panjardums and other archetypes <b>also </b>existed under the &quot;noble&quot; definition and could be either lone wandering threats/wonders or the lieutenants/courtiers/prize vassals of the lady or lord of a court.<br /> <br /> What i meant to point out is that the definitions of &quot;Raksha&quot;, &quot;noble&quot; and other elements (as established in late 1e with E:FF) changed with the present edition and most of the lesser things/servitors while still fey, are not considered - and have no potential/expectation to become - Raksha anymore and more might be done in the future to emphasize the differences from goblins, silverwights and others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T13:53:45-07:00","id":1406505,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406403#post1406403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it's perfectly suitable, necessary even, to draw on inspiration from broad, archetypal stories to find what any given raksha is about; it just goes a step too far to have them consciously, deliberately acting in emulation of stories. I like the idea that there's ultimately a measure of artifice and play-acting to what they do, particularly in relation to one another, but it takes me out of it when an intro fiction has them stand up and describe that artifice outright.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Have you read &quot;Witches Abroad&quot; by Terry Pratchett? This is the kind of creepy I was aiming for. (though by necessity. the raksha will need to be subtler)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23788-braininthejar","name":"Braininthejar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T13:53:45-07:00","id":1406506,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406403#post1406403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it's perfectly suitable, necessary even, to draw on inspiration from broad, archetypal stories to find what any given raksha is about; it just goes a step too far to have them consciously, deliberately acting in emulation of stories. I like the idea that there's ultimately a measure of artifice and play-acting to what they do, particularly in relation to one another, but it takes me out of it when an intro fiction has them stand up and describe that artifice outright.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Have you read &quot;Witches Abroad&quot; by Terry Pratchett? This is the kind of creepy I was aiming for. (though by necessity. the raksha will need to be subtler)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23788-braininthejar","name":"Braininthejar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T13:53:45-07:00","id":1406507,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406403#post1406403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it's perfectly suitable, necessary even, to draw on inspiration from broad, archetypal stories to find what any given raksha is about; it just goes a step too far to have them consciously, deliberately acting in emulation of stories. I like the idea that there's ultimately a measure of artifice and play-acting to what they do, particularly in relation to one another, but it takes me out of it when an intro fiction has them stand up and describe that artifice outright.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Have you read &quot;Witches Abroad&quot; by Terry Pratchett? This is the kind of creepy I was aiming for. (though by necessity. the raksha will need to be subtler)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23788-braininthejar","name":"Braininthejar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T13:58:26-07:00","id":1406508,"title":"","content":"What has the forum done? I didn't know it was even possible to get a triple post... -_-","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23788-braininthejar","name":"Braininthejar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T16:18:31-07:00","id":1406523,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay#post1406183\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wanted to use a fay in a scenario, probably the manipulative kind.<br /> <br /> I managed to get my hands on some fluff, but don't quite get the feel of how they should work mechanically (the 2E stuff revolves around character traits that no longer exist, and 3E only gave us a couple of bestiary entries.)<br /> <br /> From what I see they look more or less like this:<br /> <br /> Creatues from the primal chaos, stuck into more or less stable forms, in a love-hate relationship with reality.<br /> Very high stats that are very hard to further improve.<br /> Don't lie. (why? do they need to remain true to themselves to maintain a stable 'self'?)<br /> Feed on personality traits by making their victims turn back on them (seducing the pious, scaring the brave, etc.) gradually lobotomising the victim in the process. (how would that work in 3E? would they erode principles?)<br /> Super rich, capable of spinning dreams into gold if they need to.<br /> Obsessed with narrative devices, as they are the most intuitive thing for them in reality. Try to pusposefully become Mary Sue.<br /> <br /> - What happens if they are killed in Creation? Does it just kill them, or do they need to be finished off in some special way?<br /> - Where are they power level-wise compared to exalted?<br /> - Beyond the abilities described in the bestiary examples, are there any other iconic things they should be able to do?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Here is a good rule of thumb, build a fae personality like you would a narcissist. They are the absolute core of their story and see everyone and everything else as either accessories or supporting characters to their stories<br /> <br /> they are completely capable of lies, just not ones against their nature with the exception of oaths. a fae will maintain an oath with absolute certainty, good luck getting them to make one and it normally takes some form of trickery<br /> <br /> I've never played a fae so I'm not super sure how to nail their powers down, though I have read they can be as strong as solars in some circumstances<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/359-dezeroth","name":"Dezeroth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar359_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"496"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T18:11:26-07:00","id":1406539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406430#post1406430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The term noble when it comes up in 3e seems to apply only to the ruling lord or lady of a Raksha court.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Well, we have the Spear-Empress with a retinue of twelve princes. I expect a single court can still hold several nobles.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I don't actually want the <u>term</u> commoner to be used myself. I think it carries baggage. And while I like the idea of them being drawn into the retinues of the nobility, acting as courtiers and men-at-arms, I think there should be a focus also on raksha that act separated from those, whom I imagine more commonly represented in the form of the broader tribal groups archetypal to each Direction. The sense that when most people in the North encounter the Winter Folk, even in hunting parties, they're not meeting raksha who have entered the service of their princes.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">It may yet end up retaining that holdover from Changeling: the Dreaming; that the majority of the nobility of the Fair Folk are far removed, the majority independent in their abandonment. Possibly even a meeting point between the current impulse to have the raksha on the whole be more anthropomorphic and some old interesting ideas about the unshaped.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">To whit: the likes of Adeimantus may have spent much of the Realm era more further removed in the Wyld, their estates more deeply embedded in the chaos that would bend to their will while the shallows and Creation itself is haunted by the refugees of the Balorian Crusade. Yet even the nobles are being pushed into marginal territory by the onset of the hannya. The irony of the Spear-Empress may be that she herself is fleeing a terror that nips at her heels. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Such was <b>not</b> the case in previous editions, where Cataphractoi, Eshu, Panjardums and other archetypes <b>also </b>existed under the &quot;noble&quot; definition</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Really, it depends on which part of those Editions you looked at. A lot of the terminology didn't really seem to be applied outside of the Fair Folk books, and in cases terms like cataphract did indeed seem to be used to describe a more common variety of warrior fae.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Braininthejar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406507#post1406507\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Have you read &quot;Witches Abroad&quot; by Terry Pratchett? This is the kind of creepy I was aiming for. (though by necessity. the raksha will need to be subtler)</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, a long time ago. I would actually find Lilith's attachment to and dependence upon her mirror as resonant with what seems to be being set up as a prominent motif of powerful raksha; after all, the mythos of Maja of the Starling Song is also focused on a relationship with a mirror.<br /> <br /> I would find much of what Lilith did in that kingdom and the expectations she had of people to be reminiscent of what some kinds of raksha are like when they assume a position of high authority in a country.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-07T05:00:44-07:00","id":1406742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406539#post1406539\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Well, we have the Spear-Empress with a retinue of twelve princes. I expect a single court can still hold several nobles.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I don't actually want the <u>term</u> commoner to be used myself. I think it carries baggage. And while I like the idea of them being drawn into the retinues of the nobility, acting as courtiers and men-at-arms, I think there should be a focus also on raksha that act separated from those, whom I imagine more commonly represented in the form of the broader tribal groups archetypal to each Direction. The sense that when most people in the North encounter the Winter Folk, even in hunting parties, they're not meeting raksha who have entered the service of their princes.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>I guess that can be a matter of perception or perspective, as the celtic, german &amp; norse people whose mixed folklore inspired the imagery of wild hunts had their own forms of nobility. But then it's a matter of delving into and tinkering with imagery and how elements of those words may inspire or restrict us in different ways.<br /> <br /> That said, i think we can certainly agree the definitions used in previous editions - and how those terms related to mechanical aspects of the fae - has definitely changed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-07T05:21:50-07:00","id":1406746,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406742#post1406742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I guess that can be a matter of perception or perspective, as the celtic, german &amp; norse people whose mixed folklore inspired the imagery of wild hunts had their own forms of nobility.</div> </div> </div> </div>What are you getting at?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-07T07:18:08-07:00","id":1406761,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406746#post1406746\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What are you getting at?</div> </div> </div> </div>You mentioned hunting parties when speaking of fae separate from noble retinues, but for me they evoke fox-hunts and other things that map out <b>directly </b>to nobles at play.<br /> I found it curious how the same image resonates to different things for different people - well, sometimes they resonate differently for the same person at different times - but honestly not aiming at anything in particular. <br /> <br /> But yeah, definitely rambling here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-07T17:37:08-07:00","id":1406864,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1406761#post1406761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You mentioned hunting parties when speaking of fae separate from noble retinues, but for me they evoke fox-hunts and other things that map out <b>directly </b>to nobles at play.</div> </div> </div> </div>People besides nobility have hunted, even with a degree of ceremony, and the raksha in Creation still need to eat.<br /> <br /> Winter Folk hunting parties will enjoy themselves sure, but they want your warm breath so that the stasis of the house of the Primordials doesn't turn them to lifeless ice.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-09T08:40:49-07:00","id":1407143,"title":"","content":"The raksha find the Creation-born incredibly frustrating to deal with. They can take things from raksha, without needing to tell a big story about it, and the things stay taken! Ooo! They can hurt raksha just by hitting them with things! And the hurts don't just go away! Ooo! And they can even kill raksha just by doing it, no big story, no complicated dramas, no anything, and the raksha <i>stays dead!</i> That is just so unfair! OOOO!!<br /> <br /> Basically, raksha are emotionally stunted narcissists who live in an endless free-form LARP, and take poorly to being smacked over the head with a rulebook (which Creation does all the time).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1184-ladylens","name":"LadyLens","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-09T08:52:02-07:00","id":1407144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LadyLens</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1407143#post1407143\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The raksha find the Creation-born incredibly frustrating to deal with. They can take things from raksha, without needing to tell a big story about it, and the things stay taken! Ooo! They can hurt raksha just by hitting them with things! And the hurts don't just go away! Ooo! And they can even kill raksha just by doing it, no big story, no complicated dramas, no anything, and the raksha <i>stays dead!</i> That is just so unfair! OOOO!!<br /> <br /> Basically, raksha are emotionally stunted narcissists who live in an endless free-form LARP, and take poorly to being smacked over the head with a rulebook (which Creation does all the time).</div> </div> </div> </div>I love it!! Great take on the raksha.<br /> <br /> I wonder if the name raksha comes from the Hindu word rakshasa.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-09T10:29:41-07:00","id":1407153,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1407144#post1407144\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I love it!! Great take on the raksha.<br /> <br /> I wonder if the name raksha comes from the Hindu word rakshasa.</div> </div> </div> </div>It absolutely does.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1184-ladylens","name":"LadyLens","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T10:23:14-07:00","id":1407327,"title":"","content":"I loved and devoured all the prior editions content, but I've found the scant info in Ex3 keeps what was cool, without being constrained by what came before, very much like the Fair Folk themselves.<br /> <br /> I like to think of them as an Nth type of spirit that should not be.<br /> The effluvia of the Wyld mixed with the sterner stuff of Creation in an amazing accident, creating something made of both, but not really belonging in either. <br /> <br /> They both are, and are not their bodies, if and when they have them.<br /> <br /> They come into being as a fully formed consciousness with an identity, but without purpose. That consciousness and identity are highly mutable, and they try and fill the void left by that lack of purpose. Being fully realized, yet never complete, they try and idle away and fill their existence with melodramatic amusement in the wyld, and leaching off the real thing in Creation.<br /> <br /> The ideas of Graces were really cool, and evocative, but ended up making all the Raksha feel far too similar and orderly. Having objects/ideals that solidify their being is <i>neat,</i> but everyone having a Sword, Cup, Ring, Heart, etc was a little too homogenous.<br /> <br /> One of the pitfalls I've seen before, and hope continues to get avoided, is that their Fantasies are just that, and can be summarily ignored with appropriate effort. It should play like kid's make believe, and one kid just going &quot;nuh-uh&quot; is never fun.<br /> <br /> One of their &quot;design principles&quot;, if you would call it that, is that their magics are generally more scene-long, or as long as you use this body you've woven, with fewer Excellency type effects. How that can shake down in Ex3 could use some finessing. Having persistently high pools without any ongoing cost is kinda dangerous in Ex3, which is part of the fun of Raksha, but could get kinda broken. Kinda splitting the difference, where you pay a cost to have the fantastically body part, and then fuel any supernatural efforts with that body with motes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Raksha are my fae-vorite.<br /> <br /> Reincarnation of magnificentmomo.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1542-morosemorgan","name":"MoroseMorgan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1542_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"778"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T11:56:05-07:00","id":1407345,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MoroseMorgan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1407327#post1407327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> One of the pitfalls I've seen before, and hope continues to get avoided, is that their Fantasies are just that, and can be summarily ignored with appropriate effort. It should play like kid's make believe, and one kid just going &quot;nuh-uh&quot; is never fun.</div> </div> </div> </div>Going by Adeimantus, I feel as though the most practical solution is to articulate their capacity to deal in forms of dream and fantasy in terms of actual Charms with concrete effects in addition to certain thematic failure states and the occasional strong-willed person shaking them off.<br /> <br /> I think the original idea of a lot of freedom to weave insubstantial illusion and have to put in effort to make them more solid and lasting is interesting, but a bit overcomplicated. I think the alternative of giving them the freedom to weave such things that all other actors are obligated to participate in is much too powerful.<br /> <br /> And I do think that there's something compelling enough about the idea that the raksha have a certain unreality to them that can constitute distinct disadvantages that it should be retained and rather central.<br /> <br /> There's certainly a careful balancing act between the desire for them to be legitimately threatening even to the Exalted and retaining that sense for exactly why genuine reality is so threatening to them.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T09:35:30-07:00","id":1407535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1406183-trying-to-understand-fay?p=1407345#post1407345\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Going by Adeimantus, I feel as though the most practical solution is to articulate their capacity to deal in forms of dream and fantasy in terms of actual Charms with concrete effects in addition to certain thematic failure states and the occasional strong-willed person shaking them off.<br /> <br /> I think the original idea of a lot of freedom to weave insubstantial illusion and have to put in effort to make them more solid and lasting is interesting, but a bit overcomplicated. I think the alternative of giving them the freedom to weave such things that all other actors are obligated to participate in is much too powerful.<br /> <br /> And I do think that there's something compelling enough about the idea that the raksha have a certain unreality to them that can constitute distinct disadvantages that it should be retained and rather central.<br /> <br /> There's certainly a careful balancing act between the desire for them to be legitimately threatening even to the Exalted and retaining that sense for exactly why genuine reality is so threatening to them.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Too true, it is a difficult needle to thread, but I think this editions acknowledgement that Raksha are a wholly separate entity from the other denizens of the wyld, you can have your what-would-previously-be-referred-to-as-Unshaped weaving the wyld to their stories on a whim, and having a similar resolution to prior editions. Raksha being the Vinegrette to the Water of Creation and the oil of Wyld should constrain their ability to weave absolute fantasy, but provide it with more staying power, IMO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Raksha are my fae-vorite.<br /> <br /> Reincarnation of magnificentmomo.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1542-morosemorgan","name":"MoroseMorgan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1542_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"778"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}